  "query_preview": "..Aloft high pressure is centered well to the east\nover Louisiana putting southeast Arizona under southwesterly \nflow. There is also an easterly wave centered just southeast of \nBig Bend. The southwesterly flow has led to a decrease in \nmoisture with GOES East total precipitable water ranging from \n1.25\" in western Pima County to 0.95\" along the AZ/NM border. \nStill enough moisture for isolated storms mainly over the higher\nterrain south and east of Tucson as evidenced on current radar \nand satellite. Expect this isolated activity to continue through\nthe afternoon and evening. Sunday the ridge starts to expand \nfurther westward and the easterly wave moves into northern \nChihuahua. This will lead to a couple degree increase in \ntemperatures compared to today. CAMs show thunderstorm activity \nto be similar to today with isolated activity south and east of\nTucson. \n\nEarly to mid next week the ridge becomes centered over Arizona\nleading to temperatures 5-8 degrees above normal through at\nleast Friday. This will lead to areas of Major HeatRisk across\nportions of southeast Arizona. An Extreme Heat Warning is in\nplace for Pima County, Pinal County, and portions of Graham and\nGreenlee Counties Monday-Friday. \n\nMoisture will continue to be around normal to a bit below normal\nfor this time of year this week. Monday will likely see an\nuptick in activity east of Tucson, especially in Cochise County,\nas the aforementioned easterly wave moves through far northern\nSonora. Even with the meager moisture, expect isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms in the afternoon and evening with storms\ncapable of locally heavy rainfall and strong wind gusts. \nTuesday and Wednesday NBM PoPs are 20-40 percent over the White \nMountains and south and west of Tucson. Thursday and Friday the \nhigh lifts a little further north putting southeast Arizona \nunder more favorable easterly flow. However ensembles are \npessimistic with moisture returns, keeping PWATs around normal. \nThis is likely because the flow around the high is compressed \nthanks to a trough in the Midwest. The easterly fetch of winds \ndoes not reach moisture rich areas near the Gulf of Mexico. The \nflow around the eastern periphery of the high turns north over \ncentral Texas into the Great Plains, which doesn't allow as deep\nof moisture to advect in. Even with this still expect an uptick\nin thunderstorm activity as models also show mid-level flow \n20-25 kts. And if the upper air pattern changes to allow a \nlonger fetch of easterlies, better moisture can move in, which \nwould increase storm chances and coverage.",
